<p><a href="https://profiles.open.ac.uk/jon-pike" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jonathan Pike</a>, a professor of philosophy at Open University who defends trans-exclusionary (aka &#8220;gender critical&#8221;) views has submitted a formal complaint about the appointment of his colleague, professor of philosophy <a href="https://profiles.open.ac.uk/sophie-grace-chappell" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sophie Grace Chappell</a>, a trans woman, to the <a href="https://2029.ref.ac.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Research Excellence Framework (REF) 2029</a> subpanel for philosophy, according to a report in <a href="https://www.timeshighereducation.com/news/gender-critical-scholar-complains-over-ref-panel-bias-fears" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>Times Higher Ed</em></a>.</p>
<p><span id="more-57019"></span></p>
<div id="attachment_57029" style="width: 684px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-57029" class=" wp-image-57029" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/thornton-soil-resting-halt.jpg" alt="" width="674" height="449" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/thornton-soil-resting-halt.jpg 1408w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/thornton-soil-resting-halt-300x200.jpg 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/thornton-soil-resting-halt-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/thornton-soil-resting-halt-768x512.jpg 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/thornton-soil-resting-halt-400x267.jpg 400w" sizes="(max-width: 674px) 100vw, 674px" /><p id="caption-attachment-57029" class="wp-caption-text">[SoiL Thornton, &#8220;Resting Halt&#8221;]</p></div>The REF exercise aims to assess the quality of research in the UK&#8217;s institutions of higher education and its ratings inform the distribution of grant funds.</p>
<p>Professor Pike, who founded a &#8220;Gender Critical Research Network&#8221; at his university, is objecting to Professor Chappell&#8217;s inclusion on the REF Panel because of harsh posts she made on social media about &#8220;gender critical&#8221; views. According to <em>Times Higher Ed</em>, he is worried about her being biased against views like his, and he &#8220;raised concerns about whether the philosophy panel would assess all kinds of outputs on their merits, regardless of their political or ideological position.&#8221;</p>
<p>The idea seems to be that we should infer from Chappell&#8217;s comments on social media, in which she expressed negative views about particular philosophical positions, that she can not be trusted to fulfill her responsibilities as a member of the REF subpanel.</p>
<p>One concern about this idea is that it seems to be a rather incautious leap from judgments about a person&#8217;s political expression in a non-academic environment to conclusions about their professional conduct (the kind of leap that people make, say, in arguing that <a href="https://dailynous.com/2021/02/26/philosopher-launches-anti-trans-website-colleagues-others-object/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Holly Lawford-Smith (Melbourne) should be disciplined by her university for her creation of a transphobic website</a>).</p>
<p>Another concern is that it appears to assume that the expression of strong negative opinions about some kinds of philosophical work is evidence of unprofessional <em>bias</em> against that work. But of course it needn&#8217;t be. The negative opinions may instead be a conclusions reached on the basis of detailed research and analysis of that work informed by expertise in that area.</p>
<p>Remarkably, Pike appears to say that Chappell needn&#8217;t actually be biased for her appointment to the subpanel to be problematic. It is enough for others, perhaps moved by well-poisoning by Pike, to <em>think</em> she is biased. According to <em>Times Higher Ed</em>, Pike claimed that &#8220;Chappell’s presence may dissuade departments from submitting gender-critical studies to the exercise which determines how £2 billion a year in quality-related (QR) funding is allocated to universities.&#8221; It &#8220;may,&#8221; but it just as plausibly &#8220;may not.&#8221;</p>
<p>The <em>Times Higher Ed </em>piece includes a few examples of Professor Chappell&#8217;s comments on Twitter. Here are two:</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>&#8220;The GC view is a tissue of mistakes, misconceptions &amp; misunderstandings, some of them deliberate, many of them bad-faith; combined with a willingness to stereotype, scaremonger, rumour-monger, demonise &amp; interpret with a studied lack of charity&#8221;</em></p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>&#8220;[The Gender Critical] position is a logical analogue of anti-semitism &amp; of homophobia&#8230;We shd respond to them as respond to anti-semites and homophobes.&#8221;</em></p>
<p>The tweets are presented without much context. It&#8217;s also worth noting that Twitter/X can be a virulently transphobic place in which people with all sorts of views behave terribly. For all we know, these remarks, which seem rather tame for the platform, could be examples of having shown considerable restraint in the face of severe harassment by online activists.</p>
<p>Assuming that Pike&#8217;s objection to Chappell&#8217;s having a place on the panel is based on principle, and is not mere anti-trans political opportunism, his position appears to be that if a philosopher has been publicly and harshly critical of a view defended by other philosophers, they should be deprived of the opportunity to participate in influential professional assessments. Spending a few moments imagining the various well-known and highly accomplished philosophers who might be disqualified on such a basis is just one way to see the problems with this position.</p>
<p>It is also worth considering what Pike&#8217;s objection seems to imply about the other people on the REF <a href="https://2029.ref.ac.uk/main-panel-d/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">philosophy subpanel</a>, which has 10 members.* Even if, contrary to fact, Pike had given us a good reason to think that Chappell would be objectionably biased in her appraisal of some research, why think this would be go unnoticed or unchallenged by her fellow panel members? Are the other panel members too ignorant, too manipulable, too cowardly, or too corrupt to point out when an evaluative report seems biased or unfair? Why think that they would be?</p>
<p>The <em>Times Higher Ed </em>article is <a href="https://www.timeshighereducation.com/news/gender-critical-scholar-complains-over-ref-panel-bias-fears" target="_blank" rel="noopener">here</a>.</p>
<p>* They are: chair Bill Child (Oxford University), deputy chair Beth Lord (University of Aberdeen), James Ladyman (University of Bristol), Pekka Väyrynen (University of Leeds), Stella Sandford (Manchester Metropolitan University), Stephen Mumford (Durham University), David Jones (University of Sheffield), Ema Sullivan-Bissett (University of Birmingham), Hallvard Lillehammer (University of Sheffield), and Sophie Grace Chappell (The Open University).</p>
